#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""@TOOL@ — one model call with no harness context, on the subscription.

Why this exists
  A Claude subagent drags tens of thousands of tokens of harness (system prompt,
  tool schemas, CLAUDE.md, skills, MCP) to the start line. A stripped
  `claude -p` starts at ~0.6K. For many small uniform judgements that start-up
  cost dominates the price of each item. This wrapper pins the stripping
  flags, runs the call in a fresh private empty directory, prints ONLY the
  answer on stdout, a counters trailer on stderr, and appends counters and a
  sha256 hash of the prompt to the usage log at
  $XDG_STATE_HOME/@TOOL@/usage.jsonl. This is not anonymization: anyone who
  holds a candidate prompt can confirm it against the hash.
  Text of the model that could not be printed as an answer is kept in a file
  under $XDG_STATE_HOME/@TOOL@/partial (see Guarantees).

Usage
  @TOOL@ -m haiku "prompt text"              prompt as a positional argument
  @TOOL@ -m fable -f brief.md                prompt from a file
  cat brief.md | @TOOL@ -m opus              prompt from stdin
  @TOOL@ -m haiku --system-file rubric.md …  rubric as the whole system prompt
  @TOOL@ -m haiku --system "Answer in English." "…"
  @TOOL@ -m fable -e high "…"                effort: low|medium|high|xhigh|max
  @TOOL@ -m haiku --schema s.json "…"        structured output (prints the JSON)
  @TOOL@ -m haiku --schema '{"type":…}' "…"  schema inline
  @TOOL@ -m haiku --json "…"                 the CLI's result document instead of the answer
  @TOOL@ -m haiku --tag triage "…"           tag for the usage log
  @TOOL@ -m haiku --allow-empty "…"          an empty answer is a valid result
  @TOOL@ --check                             one live Haiku probe: prefix size + language

  There is no default model: model and effort are chosen per task, on purpose.

Guarantees
  * subscription only — the child gets an ALLOW-listed environment (PATH, HOME,
    USER, LOGNAME, SHELL, LANG, LC_*, TZ, TMPDIR, TERM, @ENV@*).
    Every ANTHROPIC_* and CLAUDE_* name is dropped, so an API key, an OAuth
    token, ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS, CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR, CLAUDE_ENV_FILE or
    CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS cannot ride in from the calling session.
    `@TOOL@` sets that one itself, to 128000 (OUTPUT_CAP in c_lean_core.py): the
    CLI lowers the number to the upper bound of the model without a word, so
    a model whose default cap is below its bound writes up to the bound.
    Examples: haiku-4-5 gets 64000 instead of 32000, opus-5 gets 128000
    instead of 64000. Fewer continuations, fewer seams. When a result names a
    default cap above 128000 for some model, 128000 held that model down:
    `@TOOL@` then prints one WARN line (the model, its number, ours) and the fix
    is to raise OUTPUT_CAP in c_lean_core.py.
    Code loaders (NODE_OPTIONS, PYTHONPATH, LD_/DYLD_, NPM_/BUN_/DENO_) are
    refused outright: they would run a stranger's file inside the call.
    Proxy and CA names (HTTPS_PROXY, NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS, SSL_CERT_FILE …) do NOT
    travel by default either; name them in @ENV@PASS_ENV="A,B" if a machine needs
    them, and the trailer then says so. The CLI's API-key-only mode is never
    used: this call needs the subscription login.
  * no context — --tools "" --setting-sources "" --strict-mcp-config
    --disable-slash-commands --no-session-persistence, cwd = fresh private tmpdir.
    The system prompt is what --system or --system-file gave, and empty when
    neither is given (text that is whitespace only counts as none). The CLI's
    built-in system prompt, the Claude Code persona, never applies:
    --system-prompt is always passed. What is left is the floor the CLI adds
    on its own, ~0.6K input tokens (measured 2026-10-27 on CLI 2.1.311 with
    haiku: 579 with an empty system prompt; on CLI 2.1.288 the built-in one
    gave 6,598).
  * the usage log stores a sha256 hash of the prompt, never the prompt text.
    This is not anonymization: anyone who holds a candidate prompt can confirm
    it against the hash. @ENV@USAGE_LOG overrides the default log path.
    When XDG_STATE_HOME is unset or empty, ~/.local/state is used instead.
    Missing parent directories are made with mode 0700, log files with 0600.
  * the timeout is built in (--timeout SEC, at most 2592000 s = 30 days): the
    call is killed with its whole process tree, no external `timeout` command
    is needed. Without --timeout the limit depends on the effort:
        no -e, low, medium, high   1200 s
        xhigh                      3600 s
        max                        6000 s
    The numbers are generous on purpose: waiting longer is cheaper than
    losing an answer that is already paid for. One opus message that runs to
    the 128K output cap takes about 1200 s (measured: 131,688 output tokens
    in 1236 s), and the CLI goes on with an answer up to 3 times, i.e. up to
    4 messages: 6000 s covers that worst case too. An explicit --timeout
    always wins. A call killed by the timeout prints no answer (exit 124).
    The message says where the limit came from, how to raise it, and how
    many messages of the model had finished by then; what was spent is
    billed. The message that was still being written is lost; the text
    blocks that had come whole are saved to a file (see below).
  * an answer is printed whole, not from its last text block alone. The
    result document of the CLI carries ONE text block, the last one. For an
    ordinary call that is the whole answer. It is not when the model's
    message holds two text blocks (with thinking between them), and it is
    not when the CLI went on with the answer by itself. The CLI does that
        - after a message that hit the output cap,
        - after a response that was cut off mid-stream,
        - after a response with no visible text,
        - after thinking that was cut at the output cap,
    up to 3 times a call, and after each of these the model goes on with the
    same answer. @TOOL@ reads the event stream and prints every text block,
    joined in order with nothing put in between. The trailer says
    CONTINUED xN (N = how many times the CLI went on). It counts them even
    when the text came in one block, and a call that the timeout or a signal
    killed says it too. An answer of one message with several text blocks
    gets a NOTE and no CONTINUED: nothing was continued. The NOTE on stderr
    says why there are several blocks and shows every seam: its position in
    the answer and 40 characters on each side. A whitespace character can be
    lost at a seam (a newline was, in 12 of 12 measured seams after the
    output cap), and @TOOL@ does not guess it back: read the seams. --json
    prints the result document plus one key, @PIECES@, the text of each
    block; the key is there only when the blocks were joined.
    Blocks are joined only when they can be trusted to be one answer: the
    CLI took no message back, every message from the first text block to
    the last one continues the message before it in one of the four ways
    above, and the last block is the document's result. Otherwise nothing
    is joined: the `result` of the document is printed, as it was before
    @TOOL@ read the stream, a WARN says why, and every text block is saved to
    a file.
    Only the first kind of continuation has been seen live; the other three
    are read off the code of CLI 2.1.288.
  * no text the model wrote is dropped. Every text block is either printed
    on stdout or saved to a file, whenever the output of the CLI could be
    read at all: a failed call, a timeout, a stop by a signal, the limit
    wall, an empty answer, continuations that ran out, blocks that were not
    joined, a block the CLI's document left out of its `result`. The file
    goes to the directory @ENV@PARTIAL_DIR, by default
    $XDG_STATE_HOME/@TOOL@/partial (made with mode 0700, the file with 0600), and is
    named <UTC time>-<first 8 characters of the prompt's sha256>.txt, with a
    counter added when that name is taken. Its first line says what it is:
    the tool, the time, the model and effort, the reason, the prompt's
    sha256 (never the prompt). Then comes every block as it arrived, each
    after a line `===== @TOOL@: text block 2 of 3 =====` and with one newline
    added after it. One line on stderr gives the size and the full path.
    Such a file is the text of an answer that did not finish or was not
    printed, never a result, and nothing of it goes to stdout. A file that
    cannot be written costs one WARN and nothing else (none is left behind:
    a half-written one is removed, and when the home directory cannot be
    told, with no HOME and no entry for the user in the system, and no
    XDG_STATE_HOME or @ENV@PARTIAL_DIR either, nothing is written at all).
    When XDG_STATE_HOME is unset or empty, ~/.local/state is used instead;
    @ENV@PARTIAL_DIR overrides either default. An ordinary call,
    whose one text block is what was printed, writes no file. Three things
    are left out on purpose: a good call with --schema (its answer is the
    structured output, and text blocks that came with it are not kept),
    --check (a probe, not an answer), and blocks of whitespace alone.
    Thinking is never printed and never saved.
  * SIGINT/SIGTERM/SIGHUP kill the child with its tree. The exit code is
    128+signal when the signal lands before the call has started (@TOOL@ is
    still reading the prompt) or when the child itself dies of the signal.
    During a call to the claude CLI it is 137 or 1 instead, and a call the
    CLI ended on its own is reported as a plain failure, not as INTERRUPTED
    (see Known limitations). Whatever the exit code, a stopped call is
    reported as stopped or failed, never as an
    answer. Under `nohup` SIGHUP stays ignored and the child runs in a session
    of its own, out of reach of the hangup a closed terminal sends to the whole
    job (the claude CLI ends a call on it whatever it inherited), so the call
    outlives the terminal; a SIGKILL sent to the job does not reach it then,
    and the call runs to its end with nobody to read the answer.
  * `think` in the trailer is part of `out`, which is why it is printed in
    brackets: out 61 (think 55) means 61 output tokens, 55 of them thinking.
    The trailer also names the binary that ran, because it is chosen by
    @ENV@CLAUDE_BIN or by the parent's PATH.
  * an empty answer is a failure, not a result (--allow-empty turns that off):
    `@TOOL@ … > answer.md` used to leave an empty file behind and exit 0.

The limit wall (exit 75)
  It is decided by the STRUCTURE of the CLI answer, never by words in a document:
  is_error true, plus api_error_status 429 or an error type saying rate_limit.
  Only when the CLI returns no document at all may the sentence decide, and
  only where the CLI itself wrote it: on stderr, or in a line of stdout that is
  not an event of the stream (a line whose first character after leading
  whitespace is `{` is one, whole or cut short, and holds a model's words).
  A document with is_error false is never a wall, whatever the answer says and
  whatever the exit code was — "You've hit your weekly limit" as an answer is
  data, not a verdict, and a failed call that merely quotes it is a plain failure.

Known limitations
  * A wall without a structural marker (is_error true, but no api_error_status
    429 and no rate_limit error type) is an ordinary failed call: exit 1, not 75.
  * A real limit wall has never been observed live by this tool; the markers it
    looks for come from a recorded CLI envelope (see c_lean_core.py), not
    from a wall met here.
  * The output cap. It is the CLI, not @TOOL@, that continues an answer cut at
    the cap, and it does so at most 3 times (4 messages; measured on CLI
    2.1.288). When the last of them is cut too, the call fails: exit 1,
    nothing on stdout, the tokens are spent; the text of the cut messages is
    saved to a file. Thinking counts against the cap, so a message can be
    all thinking and no text. A continuation does not promise a complete
    answer either: the model may stop early in it.
  * A response stopped by a safety classifier is not joined. The CLI then
    asks the model to go on without the withheld part, and whether the model
    continues its text or starts again is not known. The same holds for any
    request of the CLI that @TOOL@ does not know, and for a call where the CLI
    took a message back: the `result` of the document is printed with a
    WARN, and every text block is saved to a file. A message the CLI took
    back without saying so in the stream cannot be told from one that
    stands.
  * With --schema nothing is joined: the structured output of the result
    document is printed as it is. CONTINUED xN in the trailer is then the
    only sign that the CLI went on with the answer, and a good call saves no
    file, whatever text blocks came with it.
  * The start window. Until the signal handlers are installed (the interpreter
    starting, the imports, parsing the flags) no call has been made. A SIGTERM
    or SIGHUP in this window (unless SIGHUP was ignored at start) ends the
    process and the OS reports the signal.
    A SIGINT there does the same, often with a KeyboardInterrupt traceback;
    inside the interpreter's own start-up it can instead end it with exit 1 and
    "Fatal Python error: init_…", and now and then CPython only reports the
    KeyboardInterrupt and the call goes on as usual.
  * The exit code of a stop. A signal that lands before the call has started
    (@TOOL@ is still reading the prompt) gives 128+signal, and no call is made.
    During a call 128+signal comes out only when the child itself dies of the
    signal, and the claude CLI does not: it catches all three.
    A signal sent to @TOOL@'s pid alone (kill PID) is seen by @TOOL@ only: its
    handler kills the child with SIGKILL, and the exit is 137 (128+9), with
    INTERRUPTED in the trailer. A signal sent to the whole process group
    (Ctrl-C, a closed terminal, kill -- -PGID) reaches the CLI too, and the
    CLI ends the call itself tens of milliseconds later (SIGINT: exit 0 with
    an error_during_execution document; SIGTERM: 143; SIGHUP: 129). If that
    comes before the handler's SIGKILL, @TOOL@ reports an ordinary failure,
    exit 1 ("claude reported failure (rc=0 subtype=error_during_execution)"
    or "the stream of claude ended with no `result` event (… rc=129)"); if
    not, 137. When the child is in a
    session of its own (SIGHUP ignored at start, see Guarantees), a SIGINT or
    SIGTERM sent to the job reaches @TOOL@ alone: 137. After a stop of your own,
    read 1 and 137 as "stopped, no answer", not as a failed prompt.
  * Exit 120. When stdout or stderr can no longer be written (a closed
    terminal, or a pipe whose reader has exited), the next write there fails.
    The interpreter then cannot flush its streams on the way out, and @TOOL@
    exits 120 whatever the call gave. The trailer goes to stderr before the
    answer goes to stdout, so when stderr is the stream that fails, the
    answer is not printed either; the usage-log line is written all the same,
    and so is the file of text blocks of a call that saves them (it is
    written before anything is printed). A good call saves nothing, so its
    answer is lost with the stream.
    Send both streams to files when the terminal may close or the reader may
    go away.
  * SIGQUIT (Ctrl-\\) and every other fatal signal @TOOL@ does not catch end it
    on the spot: no trailer, no usage-log line. A child the signal did not
    reach (kill -QUIT PID) runs its call to the end, and the answer is lost.

Exit codes
  0 ok · 1 the call failed (an empty answer counts, an answer still cut off
    after the CLI's last continuation counts, and so does a call the claude
    CLI ended on its own after a stop signal, see Known limitations) ·
  64 usage (a bad flag value included) ·
  69 the claude binary cannot be run (missing, no +x, bad format) ·
  75 LIMIT WALL — not an ordinary failure · 124 timeout ·
  120 stdout or stderr could no longer be written: a closed terminal, or a pipe
    whose reader has exited (see Known limitations) ·
  137 the child was killed with SIGKILL, as a rule by @TOOL@'s own handler on a
    stop signal (see Known limitations) ·
  130/143/129 stopped by SIGINT/SIGTERM/SIGHUP
    — before the call starts, or when the child itself dies of the signal
    (see Known limitations)
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import math
import os
import sys

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
import c_lean_core as B  # noqa: E402

__doc__ = (__doc__.replace("@TOOL@", B.TOOL_NAME).replace("@ENV@", B.ENV_PREFIX)
           .replace("@PIECES@", B.PIECES_KEY))

CHECK_MAX_INPUT = 1500
CHECK_SYSTEM = ("You are a terse evaluator. Always answer in English. "
                "Reply with exactly one word and nothing else.")
CHECK_PROMPT = "Reply with the single word: OK"
SEAM_CONTEXT = 40     # characters shown on each side of a seam in the NOTE


class Parser(argparse.ArgumentParser):
    def error(self, message):
        # argparse exits 2 on a value it cannot parse; 64 is what the help promises
        print(f"{B.TOOL_NAME}: %s" % message, file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(B.EXIT_USAGE)


def build_parser():
    p = Parser(
        prog=B.TOOL_NAME, add_help=False,
        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter,
        description=__doc__)
    p.add_argument("prompt", nargs="*", help=argparse.SUPPRESS)
    p.add_argument("-f", "--file", dest="files", action="append", default=[],
                   help="read the prompt from FILE (repeatable)")
    p.add_argument("-m", "--model", help="fable | opus | sonnet | haiku | full id (no default)")
    p.add_argument("-e", "--effort", help="low | medium | high | xhigh | max")
    p.add_argument("--system", help="system prompt text")
    p.add_argument("--system-file", help="system prompt from a file")
    p.add_argument("--schema", help="JSON Schema: a file path or inline JSON")
    # No default here: None means "not given", and the limit then comes from
    # the effort (B.timeout_plan). A number here could not be told from one
    # the operator typed.
    p.add_argument("--timeout", type=float, default=None,
                   help="seconds for the whole call, must be > 0 (default: 1200, "
                        "3600 at -e xhigh, 6000 at -e max)")
    p.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", help="print the CLI's result document")
    p.add_argument("--allow-empty", action="store_true",
                   help="an empty answer is a valid result (default: a failure)")
    p.add_argument("--tag", default="", help="tag written to the usage log")
    p.add_argument("--backend", default="claude", help="model backend (only 'claude' for now)")
    p.add_argument("--check", action="store_true", help="one live Haiku probe, then exit")
    p.add_argument("-h", "--help", action="store_true")
    return p


def gather_prompt(args, parser) -> str:
    parts = []
    if args.prompt:
        parts.append(" ".join(args.prompt))
    for path in args.files:
        if path == "-":
            parts.append(sys.stdin.read())
        else:
            parts.append(B.read_file_arg("-f", path))
    if not parts and not sys.stdin.isatty():
        parts.append(sys.stdin.read())
    text = "\n\n".join(x for x in parts if x.strip())
    if not text.strip():
        print(f"{B.TOOL_NAME}: empty prompt (give text, -f FILE, or pipe stdin)", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(B.EXIT_USAGE)
    return text


def gather_system(args) -> str:
    if args.system and args.system_file:
        print(f"{B.TOOL_NAME}: use --system or --system-file, not both", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(B.EXIT_USAGE)
    if args.system_file:
        return B.read_file_arg("--system-file", args.system_file)
    return args.system or ""


def do_check(args) -> int:
    # the probe is always haiku at effort low, whatever -e says
    timeout, _source = B.timeout_plan(args.timeout, "low")
    r = B.call_model(CHECK_PROMPT, system=CHECK_SYSTEM, model="haiku", effort="low",
                     timeout=timeout, backend=args.backend, tag="check", tool=B.TOOL_NAME + " --check")
    sys.stderr.write(B.trailer(r, B.TOOL_NAME + " --check"))
    # the answer as `c-lean` would print it: every block joined, else the result
    seen = (B.splice(r.pieces)[0] if r.pieces else r.text) or ""
    probe = {"ts": B.now_iso(), "kind": "check", "exit": r.exit, "wall": bool(r.wall),
             "usage": r.usage, "total_in": r.total_in, "secs": round(r.secs or 0, 2),
             "answer": seen[:200], "err": (r.err or "")[:300],
             "tokens_unknown": bool(r.tokens_unknown),
             "raw_keys": sorted((r.raw or {}).keys()) if isinstance(r.raw, dict) else None,
             "raw_usage": (r.raw or {}).get("usage") if isinstance(r.raw, dict) else None}
    probe_path = os.environ.get(B.ENV_PREFIX + "CHECK_PROBE_FILE")
    if probe_path:
        with open(probe_path, "a") as f:
            f.write(json.dumps(probe, ensure_ascii=False) + "\n")

    fails = []
    if r.wall:
        print(f"{B.TOOL_NAME} --check: LIMIT WALL — %s" % r.err, file=sys.stderr)
        return B.EXIT_WALL
    if r.interrupted:
        print(f"{B.TOOL_NAME} --check: interrupted — %s" % r.err, file=sys.stderr)
        return r.exit or B.EXIT_FAIL
    if not r.ok:
        fails.append("call failed: %s" % r.err)
    elif not seen.strip():
    # --check exists to prove the pipe works before a paid call; an empty
        # answer is the pipe not working, and it used to print OK.
        fails.append("empty answer — not a result")
    if r.tokens_unknown or (r.ok and r.total_in == 0):
        # The whole point of --check is the input-token number. When it cannot be
        # read, `0 > 1500` is false and the guard reports OK — wide open in
        # exactly the case it exists for (the CLI changing the shape of `usage`).
        fails.append("usage counters unreadable (in %s cw %s cr %s out %s) — the prefix "
                     "cannot be verified, so this is NOT a pass"
                     % (r.usage.get("in"), r.usage.get("cw"), r.usage.get("cr"),
                        r.usage.get("out")))
    if r.total_in > CHECK_MAX_INPUT:
        fails.append("prefix too fat: %d input tokens (in %s + cw %s + cr %s) > %d — "
                     "the CLI probably grew a default context; re-measure"
                     % (r.total_in, r.usage.get("in"), r.usage.get("cw"), r.usage.get("cr"),
                        CHECK_MAX_INPUT))
    if r.ok and not B.looks_english(seen):
        fails.append("answer is not English (%r) — a language setting is leaking in" % seen[:60])
    for f in fails:
        print(f"{B.TOOL_NAME} --check: FAIL %s" % f, file=sys.stderr)
    if fails:
        return B.EXIT_FAIL
    print(f"{B.TOOL_NAME} --check: OK · haiku · %d input tokens (limit %d) · answer %r · %.1fs"
          % (r.total_in, CHECK_MAX_INPUT, seen[:40], r.secs or 0), file=sys.stderr)
    return B.EXIT_OK


def note_lines(r, answer, seams) -> list:
    """The NOTE for an answer put together from several text blocks: why there
    are several, and every seam. Nothing is put in at a seam, so the reader
    has to see the places where a space or a newline may have been lost."""
    if r.continued:
        after = []
        for kind in r.went_on:
            if kind not in after:
                after.append(kind)
        why = ("the CLI continued the answer %d time%s (after %s), and its result document "
               "carries only the last text block"
               % (r.continued, "" if r.continued == 1 else "s",
                  ", ".join("%s%s" % (k, (" x%d" % r.went_on.count(k))
                                      if r.went_on.count(k) > 1 else "") for k in after)))
    else:
        # one message, several text blocks: nothing was continued, and the
        # trailer must not say so
        why = ("the answer came in %d text blocks of one message, and the result document of "
               "the CLI carries only the last of them" % len(r.pieces))
    if not seams:
        return [f"{B.TOOL_NAME}: NOTE %s; all of the visible text is in one block, so nothing was "
                "joined" % why]
    lines = [f"{B.TOOL_NAME}: NOTE %s: %d pieces are printed joined exactly as they came. Nothing is put "
             "in at a seam, so a space or a newline may be missing there — check each one:"
             % (why, len(seams) + 1)]
    for n, pos in enumerate(seams, 1):
        # JSON strings: a newline shows as \n, and the edges of the context are visible
        lines.append(f"{B.TOOL_NAME}: NOTE seam %d after %d characters: %s | %s"
                     % (n, pos,
                        json.dumps(answer[max(0, pos - SEAM_CONTEXT):pos], ensure_ascii=False),
                        json.dumps(answer[pos:pos + SEAM_CONTEXT], ensure_ascii=False)))
    return lines


def to_keep(r) -> list:
    """The text blocks of the call that are worth a file: all of them, unless
    there is no visible text in any."""
    return r.blocks if any(b.strip() for b in r.blocks) else []


def settle(args, r, schema, timeout, limit_source):
    """What the call comes to, with nothing printed yet: (exit code, lines for
    stderr after the trailer, text for stdout or None, why its text blocks are
    not all on stdout or None when they are)."""
    say = []
    if r.wall:
        say.append(f"{B.TOOL_NAME}: LIMIT WALL — stop and wait for the window to reset. %s" % r.err)
        return B.EXIT_WALL, say, None, r.why or "the call hit the limit wall"
    if r.interrupted:
        say.append(f"{B.TOOL_NAME}: stopped — %s" % (r.err or "interrupted"))
        return r.exit or B.EXIT_FAIL, say, None, r.why or "the call was stopped by a signal"
    if r.exit == B.EXIT_TIMEOUT:
        say.append(f"{B.TOOL_NAME}: %s" % B.timeout_message(timeout, limit_source, r.finished,
                                                  len(to_keep(r))))
        return B.EXIT_TIMEOUT, say, None, r.why or "the call was killed by the timeout"
    if not r.ok:
        say.append(f"{B.TOOL_NAME}: %s" % (r.err or "failed"))
        return r.exit or B.EXIT_FAIL, say, None, r.why or "the call failed"
    if r.warn:
        say.append(f"{B.TOOL_NAME}: WARN %s" % r.warn)
    # What goes to stdout: every text block of an answer that came in several,
    # or the `result` of the document, which is the last one.
    answer, seams = B.splice(r.pieces) if r.pieces else (r.text, [])
    if r.pieces:
        say += note_lines(r, answer, seams)
    # Which text blocks are NOT on stdout once this is printed. Joined pieces
    # are all there. Without them stdout gets the `result` of the document,
    # and that covers one block which is that `result` and nothing else: not
    # blocks that were not joined, and not a block the document left out (the
    # CLI leaves `result` empty when the last block of the answer is not text).
    unprinted = None
    if not r.pieces:
        if len(r.blocks) > 1:
            unprinted = ("%d text blocks came and were not joined: only the `result` of the "
                         "CLI's document was printed" % len(r.blocks))
        elif r.blocks and r.blocks[0].strip() != r.text:
            unprinted = ("the only text block of the call is not the `result` of the CLI's "
                         "document, and the `result` is what was printed")
    empty = not answer.strip() and r.structured is None and not args.allow_empty
    if empty:
        # `c-lean … > answer.md` with an empty result left an empty file and exit 0,
        # so the next step of the pipeline counted the step as done.
        say.append(f"{B.TOOL_NAME}: empty answer — not a result (use --allow-empty if a blank "
                   "answer is legitimate for this rubric)")
        if not args.json:
            return (B.EXIT_FAIL, say, None,
                    "the `result` of the CLI's document is empty, and nothing was printed")
        # --json asked for the CLI document, and the empty answer is precisely
        # what it is needed to explain (cut off? refused? a wall?). Report the
        # failure, but do not swallow the thing that was asked for.

    if args.json:
        # the document as the CLI gave it; its `result` is the last text block
        # only, so the blocks of a joined answer ride along under one key. No
        # key when nothing was joined: the document then is what was printed.
        doc = dict(r.raw, **{B.PIECES_KEY: r.pieces}) if r.pieces else r.raw
        out = json.dumps(doc, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2) + "\n"
    elif schema:
        if r.structured is None:
            say.append(f"{B.TOOL_NAME}: --schema given but the CLI returned no structured_output")
            return B.EXIT_FAIL, say, r.text + "\n", unprinted
        out = json.dumps(r.structured, ensure_ascii=False) + "\n"
    else:
        out = answer + "\n"
    if empty:
        return B.EXIT_FAIL, say, out, unprinted
    # A good call under a schema keeps nothing: its answer is the structured
    # output, whatever text came with it.
    return B.EXIT_OK, say, out, (None if schema else unprinted)


def keep(r, prompt, reason) -> str:
    """Save the text blocks that are not going to stdout and return the line
    that says so. A file that cannot be written costs a warning and nothing
    else: the exit code and the rest of the output stay what they were."""
    blocks = to_keep(r)
    size = ("%d characters in %d text block%s"
            % (sum(len(b) for b in blocks), len(blocks), "" if len(blocks) == 1 else "s"))
    try:
        path = B.save_blocks(blocks, prompt, reason, model=r.model, effort=r.effort)
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 — keeping the text must not break the report of the call
        try:
            where = B.partial_dir()
        except OSError:  # the place itself is what cannot be told
            where = B.ENV_PREFIX + "PARTIAL_DIR"
        return (f"{B.TOOL_NAME}: WARN could not save %s to %s: %s — that text is lost"
                % (size, where, getattr(e, "strerror", None) or e))
    return (f"{B.TOOL_NAME}: SAVED %s to %s — the text of an answer that did not finish or was not "
            "printed, NOT a result" % (size, path))


def main(argv=None) -> int:
    parser = build_parser()
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    if args.help:
        sys.stdout.write(__doc__)
        return B.EXIT_OK
    timeout, limit_source = B.timeout_plan(args.timeout, args.effort)
    if not math.isfinite(timeout) or timeout <= 0:
        # "0 = no limit" is the natural guess and it was a trap: the call was
        # killed the moment it started. `nan` and `inf` pass a plain `<= 0` test
        # and then blow up inside select() with a ValueError/OverflowError.
        print(f"{B.TOOL_NAME}: --timeout must be > 0 seconds and finite (there is no 'unlimited' "
              "value; nan and inf are not numbers of seconds)", file=sys.stderr)
        return B.EXIT_USAGE
    if timeout > B.MAX_TIMEOUT:
        print(f"{B.TOOL_NAME}: --timeout is at most %d seconds (30 days)" % B.MAX_TIMEOUT, file=sys.stderr)
        return B.EXIT_USAGE
    if args.backend not in B.BACKENDS:
        print(f"{B.TOOL_NAME}: unknown --backend %r (have: %s)"
              % (args.backend, ", ".join(sorted(B.BACKENDS))), file=sys.stderr)
        return B.EXIT_USAGE
    B.install_signal_handlers()
    if args.check:
        return do_check(args)
    if not args.model:
        print(f"{B.TOOL_NAME}: -m MODEL is required (fable | opus | sonnet | haiku | full id); "
              "there is no default on purpose", file=sys.stderr)
        return B.EXIT_USAGE

    try:
        prompt = gather_prompt(args, parser)
        system = gather_system(args)
        schema = B.read_text_arg(args.schema) if args.schema else None
    except B.BadArg as e:
        print(f"{B.TOOL_NAME}: %s" % e, file=sys.stderr)
        return B.EXIT_USAGE
    # No warning for a missing system prompt: it is passed empty, and the CLI's
    # built-in one never applies. Measured 2026-10-03 on haiku, CLI 2.1.288:
    # 406 input tokens a call with an empty system prompt, 6,598 with the
    # built-in one (10 in + 6,588 cache write), which is what a call without
    # --system used to cost under a WARN line.

    r = B.call_model(prompt, system=system, model=args.model, effort=args.effort,
                     schema=schema, timeout=timeout, backend=args.backend,
                     tag=args.tag, tool=B.TOOL_NAME)
    code, say, out, unprinted = settle(args, r, schema, timeout, limit_source)
    # The file comes first, before a single line is printed: a stream that can
    # no longer be written (see Exit 120 in the help) must not cost the text too.
    if unprinted and to_keep(r):
        say.append(keep(r, prompt, unprinted))
    if B.cap_over_note(r):
        say.append(f"{B.TOOL_NAME}: WARN %s" % B.cap_over_note(r))
    sys.stderr.write(B.trailer(r))
    for line in say:
        print(line, file=sys.stderr)
    if out is not None:
        # a lone surrogate (a half of a pair the model wrote) cannot be encoded
        # to UTF-8: it goes out as the escape the saved file has, not as a traceback
        sys.stdout.write(out.encode("utf-8", "backslashreplace").decode("utf-8"))
    return code


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        rc = main()
    finally:
        # the exit code is decided, on every path: a signal landing during
        # interpreter shutdown must not turn it into -2 / -15
        B.ignore_stop_signals()
    sys.exit(rc)
